WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.110
MIGUEL HERNAN: Causal
graphs are your friends.

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:08.300
And to show you why,
in this segment, I'm

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.460
going to describe a scientific
controversy from the late '70s.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:15.000
This is a debate that involved
some of the most brilliant

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.350
clinical investigators at the time.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:20.910
And by the end of this session,
when we will talk a little bit more

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:24.360
about graphs in the
next segments, you will

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.020
be able to resolve this
controversy by yourself

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:30.960
and you will be convinced that
causal graphs help us think better.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.160
The 1970s was a fascinating decade that
will be remembered for many things,

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.270
but one of the most important things
that happened during the early '70s

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:42.120
is that for the first time in
human history millions of women

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.890
were receiving estrogen after menopause.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:48.950
And this use of estrogen was
encouraged by doctors, by the media,

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.010
by popular books.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:55.620
However, investigators were very
concerned, because in 1975 and '76,

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:59.430
there were some studies that found
that women receiving estrogens

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:04.260
had a higher risk of being diagnosed
with endometrial cancer than women

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.090
not receiving estrogens.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:07.520
And the question is why?

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:09.330
There is one obvious answer--

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:13.960
possible answer -- which is
that estrogens cause cancer.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:17.010
But there were two prominent
clinical investigators

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:20.630
from Yale, Alvin Feinstein
and Ralph Horwitz,

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:22.350
and they proposed another possibility.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:26.040
They said that estrogens did
not have to cause cancer for us

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:32.410
to find this association
between estrogen use and cancer.

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:37.410
They said that what might
happen is that estrogens simply

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:41.100
lead to a diagnosis of cancers
that were already present,

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:44.460
but would not have been diagnosed
in the absence of estrogens.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:50.220
They knew that between 20 and 60% of
cancers are silent and asymptomatic,

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.860
we only find them when we look for them.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:57.630
And estrogen use causes
uterine bleeding inside women.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:01.200
So they reasoned that the
bleeding was leading doctors

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.320
to diagnose preexisting silent cancers.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.840
And as a result, estrogen users have
a higher rate of diagnosis of cancer,

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:13.420
even if estrogens did not
cause cancer by themselves.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:16.470
So what Feinstein and Horwitz
said, in their own words is,

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:19.720
"If estrogens provoke the bleeding
that leads to uterine exam,

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.620
asymptomatic uterine cancer
is much more likely to be

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:27.450
found in women receiving estrogens
than in those who do not receive it."

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.520
And they thought that
this could fully explain

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.820
the association between estrogen
use and endometrial cancer.

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:37.170
They refer to this phenomenon
as ascertainment bias.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.120
Therefore, we have two
possible explanations

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.350
for the association between
estrogen use and endometrial cancer.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:47.070
One explanation is that
estrogens simply accelerate

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:53.220
the diagnosis of existing cancers, and
two, estrogens actually cause cancer.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:56.250
Now, these two explanations
have very different implications

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.530
for clinical practice
and for public health.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:02.670
The question is how can we distinguish
between these two explanations?

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:06.060
Which one is responsible for the
association between estrogen use

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.320
and endometrial cancer?

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.940
And this is where the
debate was initiated

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:15.330
and there was some discrepancies
among investigators

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:17.580
that we're going to discuss now.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:20.970
On the one hand, the
investigators from Yale,

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:24.210
they proposed the following approach.

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:27.300
They said, well, since
estrogens make some women bleed

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.810
and therefore, more likely to be
diagnosed with preexisting cancer, when

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:34.710
we analyze data from
these studies, let's

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:37.890
restrict the data analysis
to women who bleed,

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:40.630
whether they are taking
estrogens or not.

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:45.780
If we do that, then all women in the
analysis, because they all have bled,

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:50.220
they all have the same likelihood
of being diagnosed with cancer

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:51.990
if they have an existing cancer.

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.900
And therefore, if we still find an
association between estrogen use

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.780
and cancer, it must be that
estrogens cause cancer.

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:02.640
But there were other equally
prominent investigators who disagreed.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.850
People like Hershel Jick,
Ken Rothman, and Alec Walker,

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:08.790
from Boston University
and Harvard University,

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:13.350
said that restricting to women
who bleed was not the solution.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:17.610
They argued that we would find an
association between estrogen and cancer

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:21.060
diagnosis even among
women who bleed, and even

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:24.050
if estrogens don't cause cancer.

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:26.730
As a consequence, if that
was true, then the approach

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:29.040
that was proposed by
the Yale investigators

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.700
would not truly distinguish between
the two possible explanations.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.730
We would still have some
form of ascertainment bias.

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.320
And you may be wondering
how is this possible?

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:41.490
What the Boston investigators
said, it seems counter-intuitive.

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:45.780
Because if all women selected for the
analysis experienced bleeding, then

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:50.350
there should be no association due
to the differential ascertainment.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:54.180
Well, we will return to this
question at the end of this lesson.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:59.300
By that time, you'll be able to
resolve this problem by yourself.

